SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Report of the OSCE Moscow Mechanism’s mission of experts entitled 'Report On Violations Of International Humanitarian And Human Rights Law, War Crimes And Crimes Against Humanity Committed In Ukraine Since 24 February 2022'","publisher":"OSCE Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights / Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe","publication_date":"2022-04-13","url":"https://odihr.osce.org/odihr/515868","claim":"An OSCE expert mission reported violations of international humanitarian and human rights law, including war crimes and crimes against humanity, committed in Ukraine since 24 February 2022.","locator":"title block and document metadata, lines 61-68","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official OSCE mission report with named expert mission and explicit legal characterization.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the target 2022 period beginning 24 February 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Russian Federation conduct under Putin's command authority; the report addresses state conduct, not only battlefield units.","contrary_evidence":["Russia denied wrongdoing and blamed Ukraine/NATO narratives."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Resolution 2436 (2022) - The Russian Federation’s aggression against Ukraine: ensuring accountability for serious violations of international humanitarian law and other international crimes","publisher":"Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe","publication_date":"2022-04-28","url":"https://pace.coe.int/en/files/30024/html","claim":"PACE found that Russia's war of aggression caused thousands of civilian casualties, the near-destruction of Mariupol, and severe damage to civilian infrastructure, and called for accountability for war crimes and crimes against humanity.","locator":"resolution text, lines 149-157 and 193-204","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official parliamentary resolution grounded in current war documentation and legal accountability language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns the 2022 invasion and its consequences.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Russian Federation leadership under Putin as the initiating political authority.","contrary_evidence":["Russia rejects the aggression framing and asserts defensive justification."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.4","2B.9","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Further escalation in the Russian Federation's aggression against Ukraine","publisher":"Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe","publication_date":"2022-10-13","url":"https://assembly.coe.int/nw/xml/XRef/Xref-XML2HTML-en.asp?fileid=31390","claim":"PACE described the September 2022 sham referendums and annexation move as further escalation, demanded an immediate end to Russian aggression, and called for withdrawal of Russian troops and proxies.","locator":"resolution text, lines 56-75 and 99-100","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Council of Europe assembly resolution directly addressing the 2022 annexation escalation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns late-2022 conduct and annexation.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Putin-led Russian state actions in occupied Ukrainian territory.","contrary_evidence":["Russian authorities claimed the referendums reflected local self-determination."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Recommendation 2235 (2022) - Recent challenges to security in Europe: what role for the Council of Europe?","publisher":"Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe","publication_date":"2022-06-21","url":"https://pace.coe.int/en/files/30162/html","claim":"PACE described Russia's war against Ukraine as unprovoked and unjustified, saying it undermined the European security order and reflected a coercive, force-based model of international relations.","locator":"recommendation text, line 151","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Council of Europe recommendation with explicit framing of the conflict's character.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 war and its regional effects.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Russian Federation under Putin.","contrary_evidence":["Russia portrays the war as defensive and anti-NATO."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mounting civilian deaths, mass destruction and catastrophic humanitarian situation as Russian Federation attacks Ukraine, says UN Human Rights Office","publisher":"UN Human Rights Monitoring Mission in Ukraine / OHCHR","publication_date":"2022-03-28","url":"https://ukraine.ohchr.org/en/mounting-civilian-deaths-mass-destruction-and-catastrophic-humanitarian-situation-russian-UA","claim":"OHCHR said the Russian armed attack on Ukraine had generated strong indications of serious IHL and human-rights violations, including wide-area explosive attacks in populated areas and at least 1,119 civilian deaths by 26 March 2022.","locator":"news update, lines 79-85","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN human-rights monitoring update based on field verification and contemporaneous casualty tracking.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the first month of the 2022 invasion.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Russian Federation forces operating under Putin's decision to invade.","contrary_evidence":["Russian statements deny indiscriminate attacks and blame Ukrainian forces."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UN Human Rights reports dire human rights situation seven months after the start of the Russian Federation wide-scale armed attack on Ukraine","publisher":"UN Human Rights Monitoring Mission in Ukraine / OHCHR","publication_date":"2022-09-27","url":"https://ukraine.ohchr.org/en/un-human-rights-reports-dire-human-rights-situation-seven-months-after-start-russian-UA","claim":"OHCHR reported 5,996 civilian deaths and 8,848 injuries by 25 September 2022, plus corroborated killings of hundreds of civilians by Russian armed forces in settlements they controlled, alongside widespread arbitrary detention and disappearances.","locator":"report summary, lines 80-85","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN human-rights monitoring report with quantified casualty and detention findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers the 2022 war through late September.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Russian armed forces and Russian-controlled occupation structures under Putin's command responsibility.","contrary_evidence":["Russia denies systematic abuses and contests casualty attribution."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UN Human Rights Ukraine released reports on treatment of prisoners of war and overall human rights situation in Ukraine","publisher":"UN Human Rights Monitoring Mission in Ukraine / OHCHR","publication_date":"2023-03-24","url":"https://ukraine.ohchr.org/en/UN-Human-Rights-Ukraine-released-reports-on-treatment-of-prisoners-of-war-and-overall-human-rights-situation-in-Ukraine","claim":"OHCHR said it had documented more than 8,000 civilian deaths and almost 14,000 injuries by late March 2023, with over 90 percent caused by missiles, explosive weapons, mines, or explosive remnants, and described systematic mistreatment of Ukrainian POWs.","locator":"statement, lines 83-93","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN human-rights monitoring statement summarizing verified findings over the first year of war.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Covers 2022 conduct and its immediate aftermath.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Russian Federation war conduct under Putin, with some findings also noting Ukrainian-side violations.","contrary_evidence":["The same report also documents some violations attributable to Ukrainian forces."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Intense and Lasting Harm: Cluster Munition Attacks in Ukraine","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-05-11","url":"https://www.hrw.org/report/2022/05/11/intense-and-lasting-harm/cluster-munition-attacks-ukraine","claim":"HRW found Russian armed forces used at least six types of cluster munitions in 2022 attacks that caused hundreds of civilian casualties and damaged homes, hospitals, and schools, often in populated areas.","locator":"summary and findings, lines 34-37, 50, 92-93","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed rights report with incident-level documentation and explicit civilian-harm findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 invasion year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Russian armed forces under Putin's war decision; evidence points to Russian responsibility for many attacks.","contrary_evidence":["Ukraine also used cluster munitions at least once, according to HRW."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"We Had No Choice: Filtration and the Crime of Forcibly Transferring Ukrainian Civilians to Russia","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-09-01","url":"https://www.hrw.org/report/2022/09/01/we-had-no-choice/filtration-and-crime-forcibly-transferring-ukrainian-civilians","claim":"HRW documented Russian and Russian-affiliated officials forcibly transferring Ukrainian civilians from Mariupol and Kharkiv to Russia or occupied areas and subjecting them to coercive filtration, arbitrary detention, and abuse.","locator":"summary, lines 50-56 and 59","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Investigative rights report with direct testimony and documented coercive transfer patterns.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 occupation and deportation practices.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Russian state, occupation, and affiliated forces operating under Putin's war authority.","contrary_evidence":["Russia frames these transfers as evacuations or security screening."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"A City Devastated: Documenting Loss in Mariupol, a Ukrainian City Besieged and Devastated","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-10-18","url":"https://www.hrw.org/feature/russia-ukraine-war-mariupol/damage-assessment","claim":"HRW's satellite-and-visual analysis found massive destruction in Mariupol, including 93 percent of apartment blocks, 97 percent of educational facilities, and 100 percent of hospitals damaged, with the city cut off from water and electricity from 2 March 2022.","locator":"damage assessment summary, lines 19-34 and 39-44","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Methodologically explicit geospatial damage assessment using verified imagery and third-party sources.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly reflects 2022 siege and occupation effects.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Russian assault and siege of Mariupol under Putin's war decision.","contrary_evidence":["Russia disputed civilian-targeted damage and blamed Ukrainian defenses."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ukraine: 'Like A Prison Convoy': Russia's Unlawful Transfer And Abuse of Civilians In Ukraine During 'Filtration'","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-11-10","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/eur50/6136/2022/en/","claim":"Amnesty said Russian and Russian-controlled forces committed war crimes and likely crimes against humanity by unlawfully transferring or deporting civilians and forcing them through abusive filtration that included arbitrary detention, torture, and family separation.","locator":"report title and summary, lines 67-68","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major rights organization report with direct legal characterization and abuse description.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 conduct.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Russian state and proxy forces under Putin's war and occupation authority.","contrary_evidence":["Russia denies unlawful transfer and presents filtration as security screening."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia/Ukraine: Illegitimate results of sham 'referenda' must not enable illegal annexation of occupied areas","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-09-28","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/09/russia-ukraine-illegitimate-results-of-sham-referenda-must-not-enable-illegal-annexation-of-occupied-areas/","claim":"Amnesty said the occupied-territory referendums were held at gunpoint with Russian soldiers and proxies present, were illegal under international law, and formed part of Russia's strategy of aggression and annexation.","locator":"news release, lines 63-76","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Clear contemporary NGO statement on coercion and annexation illegality.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns late-September 2022 annexation steps.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Putin-led Russian authorities and occupation structures.","contrary_evidence":["Russia claimed the referendums reflected local popular will."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.6","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia-Ukraine international armed conflict: ICRC asks for immediate and unimpeded access to all prisoners of war","publisher":"International Committee of the Red Cross","publication_date":"2022-10-14","url":"https://www.icrc.org/en/document/ukraine-russia-icrc-asks-immediate-and-unimpeded-access-to-all-prisoners-of-war","claim":"The ICRC said it had visited hundreds of POWs but had not been able to see thousands more, and stressed that immediate access to all POWs is a legal obligation under the Third Geneva Convention.","locator":"news release, lines 180-189","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct humanitarian-law statement from the organization responsible for POW access and monitoring.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 POW conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the parties to the Russia-Ukraine international armed conflict, including Russian authorities and forces.","contrary_evidence":["Access limitations affected both sides; the ICRC does not assign individual blame in this note."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia-Ukraine international armed conflict: New visits to prisoners of war bring much-awaited news to families","publisher":"International Committee of the Red Cross","publication_date":"2022-12-08","url":"https://www.icrc.org/en/document/russia-ukraine-international-armed-conflict-new-visits-prisoners-war-bring-much-awaited","claim":"The ICRC reported new POW visits by both sides, said the visits preserve humanity in the conflict, and urged more regular access to all prisoners of war.","locator":"news release, lines 180-192","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct ICRC operational update on POW access and treatment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers late-2022 POW treatment conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Russian and Ukrainian authorities as detaining powers in the conflict.","contrary_evidence":["The report does not assign full legal responsibility to one side."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Is a Russia-Ukraine war imminent?","publisher":"Chatham House","publication_date":"2022-01-26","url":"https://www.chathamhouse.org/2022/01/russia-ukraine-war-imminent","claim":"Chatham House argued that the Russian leadership sought to subdue Ukraine and turn it into a buffer zone with limited sovereignty, using coercion to secure a no-NATO outcome.","locator":"article section 'What does Russia want?', lines 417-420","source_type":"other","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Well-regarded expert analysis citing Kremlin positions and strategic objectives, but still interpretive.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Pre-invasion analysis that helps explain 2022 intent and alternatives.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Putin and the Russian leadership.","contrary_evidence":["This is analysis, not a legal finding or direct admission."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.3","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin's weakness on the frontlines of public opinion","publisher":"Chatham House","publication_date":"2022-02-28","url":"https://www.chathamhouse.org/2022/02/putins-weakness-frontlines-public-opinion","claim":"Chatham House observed that a full-scale Russian invasion would make little political sense and that a bloody invasion could not serve Russia's long-term aim of holding Ukraine within the 'Russian world'.","locator":"article body, lines 421-423","source_type":"other","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Interpretive policy analysis written just after the invasion, useful for motive assessment but not documentary proof.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly relevant to Putin's 2022 decision-making and justification logic.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Putin's invasion decision and regime calculus.","contrary_evidence":["This is analyst interpretation, not direct evidence of internal Kremlin deliberations."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.3","2B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia vs. Ukraine: How does this end?","publisher":"Brookings","publication_date":"2022-03-10","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/russia-vs-ukraine-how-does-this-end/","claim":"Brookings described Putin's February 24 action as a massive invasion disguised as a special military operation and said the Kremlin justified it with falsehoods about genocide and Ukrainian nuclear ambitions.","locator":"article body, lines 162-165","source_type":"other","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Respectable policy analysis with direct references to Putin's public justifications, but still secondary interpretation.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly addresses the 2022 invasion and its stated justifications.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Putin's decision and public rhetoric.","contrary_evidence":["This is a secondary analysis, not a primary record."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.6","2B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia's War in Ukraine: A Turning Point?","publisher":"Carnegie Endowment for International Peace","publication_date":"2022-09-13","url":"https://carnegieendowment.org/europe/strategic-europe/2022/09/russias-war-in-ukraine-a-turning-point","claim":"Carnegie noted that the Ukrainian Kharkiv counteroffensive forced a rapid, disorderly Russian retreat, exposing weak leadership structures and low morale in the Russian army.","locator":"article body, lines 79-81","source_type":"other","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Credible strategic analysis, but focused on battlefield dynamics rather than direct legal attribution.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Late-2022 military situation within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Russian armed forces under Putin's command authority.","contrary_evidence":["This is battlefield analysis and not a direct finding on legal conduct."],"lenses":["2B.8","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Explainer-Peace talks on Ukraine: Russia's red lines unchanged","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-03-30","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/explainerpeace-talks-on-ukraine-russias-red-lines-unchanged-2794877","claim":"Reuters reported that Russia and Ukraine were talking about a peace deal but remained far apart, while Putin said the operation was needed because the United States was using Ukraine to threaten Russia and Moscow had to defend against alleged persecution of Russian speakers.","locator":"explainer lead and background section, lines 406-409 and 435-440","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting summarizes negotiator positions and Putin's stated rationale, but remains secondary reporting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns March 2022 peace talks and war justifications.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Putin's public justification and the Russian negotiating position.","contrary_evidence":["Reuters also notes Ukraine's opposing view that Russia was pursuing land grab and maximalist demands."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.3","2B.6","2B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin Declares Partial Mobilization","publisher":"RFE/RL, reporting by Reuters","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://www.rferl.org/a/russia-putin-ukraine-war-partial-mobilization/32044506.html","claim":"Putin announced a partial mobilization on 21 September 2022 after seven months of war and battlefield setbacks, saying the decision followed the recommendation of the Defense Ministry and General Staff.","locator":"news item, lines 114-115","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters-reported contemporaneous mobilization announcement with direct attribution to Putin.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns 2022 wartime mobilization.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed directly to Putin as the decree issuer.","contrary_evidence":["The article notes the move was unpopular and followed Russian setbacks."],"lenses":["2B.8","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"An emboldened, confident Putin says there will be no peace in Ukraine until Russia's goals are met","publisher":"Associated Press","publication_date":"2023-12-14","url":"https://apnews.com/article/putin-russia-press-conference-moscow-ukraine-ef4e88fda50e6ad75b8a1979b95d9fcc","claim":"AP reported that Putin said there would be no peace until Russia achieves its goals, that those goals remained unchanged from the day he sent troops in February 2022, and that his 'de-Nazification' claim rested on false assertions about Ukraine.","locator":"article text, lines 2373-2375 and 2303-2305","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Directly quotes and summarizes Putin's public remarks, but the article is a later secondary account.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Retrospective but directly links 2023 rhetoric back to February 2022 objectives.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed directly to Putin's public stance and unchanged war aims.","contrary_evidence":["The article notes Kyiv and the West reject the de-Nazification claim as false."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ukraine: Mourners commemorate 8 men killed in Bucha","publisher":"Associated Press","publication_date":"2023-03-04","url":"https://apnews.com/article/ukraine-russia-war-bucha-civilians-killed-f2dfb0762ccafba59d0a442e3603edea","claim":"AP reported that eight men were killed by Russian forces in Bucha during the 2022 advance on Kyiv, with their bodies left in the street for a month before Russian withdrawal.","locator":"article text and captions, lines 1759-1786","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting from a major wire service, later revisiting a specific 2022 incident.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Recounts a specific 2022 occupation incident with direct relevance to civilian protection.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Russian forces acting under the broader 2022 invasion order.","contrary_evidence":["This is a 2023 retrospective on a 2022 event, not an original 2022 field report."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9"]}

Accepted new evidence: 22 distinct URLs across 10+ domains.

Search ledger:
- Candidates found: about 40-45 plausible documents across the search families
- Documents opened: 22 accessible documents
- Duplicates/mirrors/syndicated rewrites consolidated away: 12
- Irrelevant or out-of-period items dropped: 5
- Inaccessible items: 3
- Accepted distinct URLs: 22

Material gaps and next best directions:
1. The biggest gap is direct primary text from Kremlin speeches/decrees and annexation/mobilization orders for 2022. The browser timed out on some Kremlin pages, so the next best target is alternate access to the 21 Feb, 24 Feb, and 30 Sep 2022 transcripts and decrees.
2. UN Security Council and General Assembly verbatim records would tighten the legality and attribution trail for 24 Feb, 2 Mar, 12 Oct, and 14 Nov 2022.
3. ICC and ICJ primary pages were blocked by the browser, so alternate access to the actual orders and prosecutor statements would strengthen legal attribution.
4. IAEA Zaporizhzhia 2022 updates would add a nuclear-risk but conflict-linked coercion thread, useful for 2B.6/2B.8.
5. For lens 2B.8 specifically, the current set shows mobilization and war-cost context, but not enough ruler-specific evidence on patronage, elite reward, or military-network enrichment. That would need budget/procurement/investigative material.

No score assigned.
